# Gatherer

## Lore

Gatherers are squat, long-armed creatures that look something like a cross between a humanoid, a horned toad and a lobster, with a small humanoid pair of six-fingered hands and a larger pair of claws. They are dimensional nomads who roam from world to world in packs, staying just long enough to pick up anything interesting - usually someone else''s property. Their natural dimension-hopping and psionic tracking make them uncanny at finding whatever they set out to find. A band starts poor and, if it is successful, ends up well armed and well equipped.

The soul worm Inix found a band more than two thousand years ago and has since recruited thousands. In Cibola they are his agents, thieves and occasional spies, sent to other worlds after whatever he wants found, and they double as the city''s security officers and scouts. Gatherer-led patrols comb the Amazon and the northern islands for magical herbs, rare animals and sentient slaves, which is why gatherers are unwelcome across South America. With age a gatherer''s exoskeleton grows spines.

## GM Notes

The book does not recommend the gatherer for player characters. Most are brutal, treacherous and wholly selfish; only soul worms, gatherer chiefs and other powerful beings can lead them, and even those masters are abandoned or attacked at the first sign of weakness. A player gatherer is the exception to its kind, still an instinctive hoarder and thief, and other races across the Megaverse will take it for a rogue or a minion of something worse.

The Chief Gatherer Kastor (printed 133) is a 14th level example of the race.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'cibola-gatherer');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
